ting x equals 1  and if I put 0 in for T here you can see  I'm getting 0 okay and then if I put T  equals 1 that's 2 plus 1 which is 3 and  if I put 1 here I'm  for so you can see we're going as Tia's  getting larger and larger as it's  increasing  we're going from one zero okay over two  three four so that means that we're  going in this direction and we can just  indicate that with these little arrows  along the the line there so with  increasing values of T we're going that  direction along the line okay now the  second example we're gonna look at this  one here x equals four cosine theta plus  3y equals nine sine theta minus two now  just a quick reminder remember the  Pythagorean trig identities remember  this one here cosine squared theta plus  sine squared theta equals one well if  you do okay we're going to use this okay  to again eliminate that parameter in  this case our parameter is Theta  okay the angle theta and the way we're  going to do that is we're gonna solve  for cosine and we're gonna solve for  sine so what I mean by that is we're  gonna get the cosine by itself so in  this top equation we're going to  subtract three and divide by four so we  get cosine theta equals X minus three  divided by four same thing this bottom  equation we're going to get the sine by  itself so we're going to add two and  divide by nine so we get sine of theta  equals y plus two all divided by nine so  now what we can do is we can substitute  in place of cosine theta X minus three  divided by four the quantity squared  because this is cosine squared plus y  plus two divided by nine squared since  that's the sine of theta and that equals  one now if we take it up just one step  further simplify it a little bit more we  get X minus three squared over sixteen  plus y plus two squared over 9 squared  of course which is 81 equals one in case  they're with me so far so this is the  form of what type of a equation an  ellipse right so if we want to graph  this ellipse you can 